<article class="env-lemma" id="06P6">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06P6">Lemma <span data-tag="06P6">78.6.1</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $B$ be an algebraic space over $S$. Let $G$ be a group algebraic space over $B$. Then $G \to B$ is separated (resp. quasi-separated, resp. locally separated) if and only if the identity morphism $e : B \to G$ is a closed immersion (resp. quasi-compact, resp. an immersion). </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We recall that by Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/03KP" data-tag="03KP">67.4.7</a> we have that $e$ is a closed immersion (resp. quasi-compact, resp. an immersion) if $G \to B$ is separated (resp. quasi-separated, resp. locally separated). For the converse, consider the diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ G \ar[r]_-{\Delta _{G/B}} \ar[d] &  G \times _ B G \ar[d]^{(g, g') \mapsto m(i(g), g')} \\  B \ar[r]^ e &  G }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       It is an exercise in the functorial point of view in algebraic geometry to show that this diagram is cartesian. In other words, we see that $\Delta _{G/B}$ is a base change of $e$. Hence if $e$ is a closed immersion (resp. quasi-compact, resp. an immersion) so is $\Delta _{G/B}$, see Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/02YW" data-tag="02YW">65.12.3</a> (resp. Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/03HF" data-tag="03HF">67.8.4</a>, resp. Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/02YW" data-tag="02YW">65.12.3</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>